      "id": "github:etcd-io/raft:pull_request:479",
      "source": "github",
      "group": "distributed-systems",
      "project": "etcd-io/raft",
      "kind": "pull_request",
      "title": "log: optimize findConflictByTerm with binary search",
      "text": "The current `findConflictByTerm` performs an O(n) linear scan backward from the given index. Since log terms are monotonically non-decreasing, we can instead binary search over `[firstIndex-1, index]` to find the rightmost entry whose term does not exceed the target, reducing the worst-case complexity from O(n) to O(log n). This matters most during partition recovery, where a follower's conflicting suffix may span thousands of entries. The valid term range is `[firstIndex-1, lastIndex]`: - `firstIndex-1` is the snapshot index, whose term is still readable. - Entries below `firstIndex-1` are fully compacted and cause `ErrCompacted`. - Entries above `lastIndex` cause `ErrUnavailable`. Corner cases handled: - `index > lastIndex`: return `(index, 0)` immediately (ErrUnavailable). - `index < firstIndex-1`: return `(index, 0)` immediately (ErrCompacted). - All accessible entries exceed `term`: return `(firstIndex-2, 0)` to signal that the matching point is in compacted territory, matching the original linear-scan behavior. All existing `TestFindConflictByTerm` cases pass unchanged. Full test suite passes with `go test ./...`. Closes #180",
      "url": "https://github.com/etcd-io/raft/pull/479",
      "createdAt": "2026-07-25T12:02:49Z",
      "updatedAt": "2026-08-20T12:06:46Z",
      "timestamp": "2026-08-20T12:06:46Z",
